---
import PostLayout from '../../layouts/PostLayout.astro';
import DotStat from '../../components/blog/DotStat.astro';
import Ledger from '../../components/blog/Ledger.astro';
import PostSection from '../../components/blog/PostSection.astro';
import TerminalWindow from '../../components/TerminalWindow.astro';
import MarkdownFile from '../../components/blog/MarkdownFile.astro';
import * as claudeMd from '../../data/aether-ml-claude.md';

const runTests = `.claude/dx python3 -m unittest discover -q tests`;

const repoLayout = `$ ls aether/layers
activations.py  dropout.py  normalization.py
conv.py         linear.py   pooling.py

$ ls aether/custom_kernels
adam_kernel.py       dropout_kernel.py  pooling_kernel.py
batchnorm_kernel.py  launch_math.py     relu_kernel.py
conv_kernel          loss_kernel.py`;

const handOff = [
	'Exploring the codebase and debating tradeoffs before any code exists',
	'Implementing against the plan we agreed on',
	'Running the full suite in the GPU container',
	'Benchmark scripts and before/after numbers',
	'Re-running the same evals on CUDA and filling in the PR numbers',
	"PR descriptions, written to the repo's template",
	'Investigations, written up as a note for a fresh session',
];

const keep = [
    'Auditing the diff before I commit',
    'Moving each PR onto my RTX 3050 laptop, and merging when both machines agree',
    'Deciding what tradeoffs are worth keeping',
	'The plan, and even what choices we may not have agreed upon'
];

const code = 'rounded border border-border bg-surface px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-sm text-text';
---

<PostLayout slug="aether-ml">
	<p>
		<a
			href="/projects/aether-ml"
			class="font-semibold text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover"
			>Aether-ML</a> exists so I can understand deep learning runtimes under the hood;
		letting an agent abstract everything defeats the point. An agent and I plan out each task,
		and I own the core architectural decisions to keep my mental map of the codebase intact.
	</p>

	<PostSection n="01" title="The decisions are mine" />
	<p>
		For a non-trivial task, I start with the most capable model as a planner. I keep my own thoughts
		and ideas to myself or share just enough context so the model explores its own approach first.
		Smarter models often find cleaner paths than mine, and I don't want to anchor them to a suboptimal design.
	</p>
	<p>
		Because the repo is laid out with a predictable directory structure, <code class={code}>layers/</code>
		mapped cleanly to <code class={code}>custom_kernels/</code>, and the harness hides build
		artifacts, saved weights, and datasets by default, it navigates straight to the relevant source
		files and writes throwaway scripts to answer its own open questions before drafting a plan.
	</p>
	<div class="my-8">
		<TerminalWindow title="~/projects/Aether-ML" code={repoLayout} lang="console" />
	</div>
	<p>
		Once the draft is ready, that's where I push back hard: we go back and forth until I understand
		why there's no silver bullet, pick the best tradeoff, tell the model which compromise we're
		taking, and have it lay out the drawbacks before locking the architecture down. When the plan
		is settled, cheaper sub-agents implement the task, escalating back to the planner only when
		they hit a roadblock.
	</p>
	<p>
		The rule I hold hardest is that no agent improvises once we've agreed on a plan. If
		implementation hits a constraint the plan didn't expect, or forces a new tradeoff, the work
		stops and comes back to me. A decision settled quietly inside a diff is one I never got to make.
	</p>
	<p>
		One time, we had agreed a plan for <a
			href="https://github.com/AlexanderSoftCode/Aether-ML/blob/main/benchmarks/compare.py"
			class="font-semibold text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover"
			>a tool that compares two benchmark runs</a> and flags slowdowns. Each run times
		the training step three times, and the plan's rule flagged a change only if it was bigger than
		how far those repeats spread apart in both runs combined. To test it, the agent slowed ReLU's
		backward pass on purpose, and the rule failed in both directions.
	</p>
	<p>
		One repeat in every run came in about 0.8 ms slower than the other two. That single outlier
		made the spread wider than the real slowdown, so a 0.78 ms (7.5%) regression went unflagged.
		Where the repeats happened to land close together, the opposite happened: two runs of identical
		code got flagged for a few microseconds.
	</p>
	<p>
	    The agent stopped, and told me the digest. Its recommendation was to throw away one extra
		repeat after the warm-up and place a threshold at 2% (or 0.01 ms here). I accepted the
		tradeoff and kept it to three repeats per run, as the tool was built for iterating, and not
		precision.
	</p>
	<MarkdownFile
		title="/CLAUDE.md"
		file={claudeMd}
		linkBase="https://github.com/AlexanderSoftCode/Aether-ML/blob/main/"
		note="snapshot · Nov 2026"
	>
		This is the desktop's file. The laptop has its own copy with the same rules but no
		devcontainer: there the agent and I share a plain virtual environment.
	</MarkdownFile>

	<PostSection n="02" title="Few tests, each one sharp" />
	<p>
	    When left to their devices, agents <strong class="text-text">love</strong> writing a lot of tests.
		For all tasks, the verification harness catches the broad correctness from end-to-end training runs
		highlighted in section 06. For code getting refactored, such as a kernel rewrite for the BatchNorm layer,
		regression tests are used to catch granular errors. Expected values compared to the reference implementation,
		or tolerances never getting loosened to make a green test suite.
	</p>
	<p>
		On the dropout kernel rewrite, that meant tests named for the bug each one catches: the
		Philox generator checked against the published Random123 vectors, nothing written past the
		end of the buffer, the keep threshold at the extreme rates, and a dropped NaN coming out as
		exactly zero.
	</p>

	<PostSection n="03" title="The agent runs where I run" />
	<p>
		Aether's CuPy paths need a real GPU, and on my desktop that means <strong class="text-text">ROCm</strong> inside a
		devcontainer. A session-start hook brings that container up when a session opens. A small
		wrapper, <code class={code}>.claude/dx</code>, finds it by its devcontainer label, because the container's name
		changes on every rebuild, and runs commands as the same user my editor does.
	</p>
	<p>
		The result is the agent and I share the same machine, and "passes for the agent" means the same thing as
		"passes for me". The whole suite takes about seven seconds, so the rule is to run all of it,
		every time.
	</p>
	<div class="my-8">
		<TerminalWindow title="~/projects/Aether-ML" code={runTests} lang="bash" />
	</div>

	<PostSection n="04" title="What I hand off, what I keep" />
	<Ledger handOff={handOff} keep={keep} />
	<p>
		The agent doesn't get to automatically file or merge PRs. Everything stays in the working tree until I've read
		them with <code class={code}>git diff</code>, and I often edit them first, usually finding DRY violations, and
	    clean it up myself. Then they go on a branch and through a PR, so CI sees them before <code class={code}>main</code> does.
	</p>
	<p>
		After CI checks I don't auto-merge the code as I still need to verify parity on both GPU vendors. My desktop has
		an <strong class="text-text">RX 9070 XT</strong> and my laptop has an <strong class="text-text">RTX 3050</strong>.
		The agent once proposed reworking the convolution kernel to do more math per loop iteration, and on the 9070 XT it cleanly
		beat the previous iteration of the dweight and dinput kernels. Due to the nature of the benchmark, the 9070 XT's 64 MB of cache
		can hold the whole layer's input, so cache hits were a given. The 3050 has about 2 MB, and the cache misses make the reads go to VRAM.
	</p>
	<p>
	    Any change must pass on both machines. For kernel changes, both must retain the same relative benefits.
		This does mean I don't write kernels coupled to an architecture, but writing kernel code in this manner is out of scope
		for this project.
	</p>

	<PostSection n="05" title="One bug, start to finish" />
	<p>
		The clearest case for working this way was a silent bug, and it surfaced in a plan review. An
		earlier session had drafted a plan for mixed precision, and I asked a fresh one what it thought.
		One line of the plan said gradients stay fp32, so no loss scaling is needed. The agent checked
		that line against real training batches and it was already false.
	</p>
	<p>
		<code class={code}>Conv2d</code>'s backward pass casts incoming gradients to fp16 for the tensor-core kernels, and
		fp16 rounds anything below roughly 3e-8 to zero. At the worst layer, the median gradient was
		smaller than that. Nothing crashed and nothing warned.
	</p>
	<p>
		The agent expected it to matter more at larger
		image sizes, where the gradient reaching each pixel should be smaller, and said plainly that it
		hadn't tested that. So I had it measure the problem before anyone fixed it: three seeds, 25
		epochs, and the share of gradient values lost at each layer. The results went into a handoff
		note, not a patch.
	</p>
	<DotStat
		from="53"
		to="72"
		unit="%"
		fromLabel="64PX · BEFORE"
		toLabel="64PX · SCALED"
		caption="Mean test accuracy over three seeds, 53.17% to 72.46%, with gradients scaled by 2<sup>10</sup> before the cast. Measured on upscaled CIFAR-10 on an RX 9070 XT."
	/>
	<p>At 32px, the size I normally train at, there was no measurable difference between the two.</p>
	<p>
		By then the thread was long and my own picture of it was getting weak. When that happens I
		start a fresh session and carry over only the facts that matter, which is what the note was for.
	</p>
	<p>
		The note also recorded what I had ruled out. Clipping was out because the problem is
		underflow, and clipping only limits large values. An adaptive scale was out because it has to
		find the largest gradient on every step, and that is an extra pass in the hot path that nobody
		had measured. Given the context, the new thread planned a fix after reading the note, and it <a
			href="https://github.com/AlexanderSoftCode/Aether-ML/pull/23"
			class="font-semibold text-accent underline hover:text-accent-hover">merged</a> as a fixed scale with a
		regression test.
	</p>

	<PostSection n="06" title="Done means checked" />
	<p>
		A change isn't finished when the agent says it is, or even when the PR opens. First the full
		suite, e2e training runs, and pipeline speed benchmarks have to pass in my desktop's
		ROCm container.
	</p>
	<p>
		Fewer tests only works if the few are enough, so every new test has to be proven against
		deliberate faults. On the dropout rewrite, that meant breaking the kernel nine different ways, with faults like overlapping thread slices
		and a missing bounds guard, and confirming each break was caught by at least one test.
	</p>
	<p>
		CUDA paths like <code class={code}>cuda_fp16.h</code> can't compile on my AMD desktop, so the PR goes up with those
		checks explicitly marked unverified. I then pull the branch onto my RTX 3050 laptop, have the
		agent run the same evals on CUDA, and update the PR description with the real numbers. It
		merges only after both GPUs pass.
	</p>
	<p>
		None of these rules make the agent faster. They make its output checkable, and on a project
		whose whole point is that I understand it, that is the only kind of speed I can use.
	</p>
</PostLayout>
